--- !u!114 &1639470
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1639468}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a3f51d3d26f9aa142b0c0d2fdee5e108, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Team5.qPCR.Runtime::Team5.qPCR.NarrationController
  audioSource: {fileID: 1639471}
  captionTexts: []
  captionTitles: []
  volumeSliders: []
  muteToggles: []
  replayButtons: []
  cues:
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_NotStarted
    Caption: Press Start qPCR Lesson to begin with the prepared 96-well plate.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_NotStarted
    Caption: qPCR copies a selected DNA region while measuring fluorescence during
      cycling. Team 5 starts with a prepared 96-well plate. Your task is to inspect
      it, load it, programme the instrument, run the lesson and explain the result.
      Use WHY at any step to learn the reason behind the action.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_InspectPlateId
    Caption: Read the highlighted Plate ID. If it matches the received experiment
      record, press Confirm Plate ID.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_InspectPlateId
    Caption: 'WHY CHECK THE ID?

      A plate has 96 wells: rows A-H and columns
      1-12. The label must match the handoff record so results are assigned to the
      correct experiment. Our record is T4-QPCR-2026-05. If it does not match, stop
      and resolve the discrepancy; do not guess.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_InspectA1Marker
    Caption: Locate the highlighted A1 corner. If the reference corner is clearly
      identified, press Confirm A1 Orientation.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_InspectA1Marker
    Caption: 'WHY FIND A1?

      A1 means row A, column 1. It fixes the direction
      of the plate map. Rotating the plate 180 degrees puts samples under the wrong
      well names. Find the red corner now, and match it to the red corner on the
      holder during loading.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_InspectOpticalSeal
    Caption: Visually inspect the highlighted seal. If it is flat, secure and undamaged,
      press Confirm Seal.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_InspectOpticalSeal
    Caption: 'WHY AN OPTICAL SEAL?

      A flat, intact seal keeps reactions enclosed
      and reduces evaporation during heating. It must transmit the light used for
      fluorescence measurements. Check edges and avoid touching the optical surface.
      If the seal is damaged or lifting, resolve it before loading.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_InspectBubbles
    Caption: Look across the highlighted wells. If no visible bubbles are present,
      press Confirm No Bubbles.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_InspectBubbles
    Caption: 'WHY CHECK BUBBLES?

      Bubbles can disturb optical readings. Team
      5 receives an already prepared and sealed plate. If bubbles are present, follow
      the approved brief centrifugation procedure with a balanced plate centrifuge
      before loading. This lesson''s plate is already ready; do not open it or remix
      its reactions.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_ReturnPlate
    Caption: Move the plate back to its blue home marker and release it.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_ReturnPlate
    Caption: 'WHY RETURN THE PLATE?

      Put the sealed plate flat in its clean
      handoff position while preparing the machine. Carry it by its edges. A floor
      drop can damage or compromise it: the simulator leaves it on the floor and
      blocks loading until Reset provides a fresh training plate.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_PowerOn
    Caption: Press the illuminated power button on the qPCR instrument.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_PowerOn
    Caption: 'WHY POWER ON?

      The electronics, optics and temperature controls
      must be ready before loading. A real instrument may perform startup checks;
      follow its manual. This simulator uses a short startup sound and welcome screen.
      The power button does not start amplification.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_OpenDrawer
    Caption: Press the Drawer Open button and wait until the drawer stops moving.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_OpenDrawer
    Caption: 'WHY OPEN THE COMPARTMENT?

      The plate holder exposes the thermal
      block, which heats and cools the reactions. Wait for the drawer to stop moving.
      Never force it. Keep the sealed plate level and identify the holder''s red
      A1 corner before inserting it.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_LoadPlate
    Caption: Grab the plate, align A1 with the socket marker, place it in the holder,
      then release it.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_LoadPlate
    Caption: 'WHY ALIGN AND SEAT?

      Match both A1 corners and lower the plate
      flat into the holder. Correct seating provides contact with the temperature-controlled
      block and preserves the well map. Release Grip only when the guide turns green.
      Amber means misaligned; red explains a rejected attempt.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_CloseDrawer
    Caption: Press Drawer Close after the plate is securely seated.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_CloseDrawer
    Caption: 'WHY CLOSE THE COMPARTMENT?

      The plate must stay securely seated
      before the heated lid and thermal programme operate. The lid helps limit condensation;
      the enclosure supports consistent temperature and optical measurement. The
      simulator locks this action until seating succeeds.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_ConfigureProtocol
    Caption: Select every field and enter its displayed guided value. Confirm each
      entry before validating the protocol.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_ConfigureProtocol
    Caption: 'WHY PROGRAM THE RUN?

      The programme controls temperature, duration
      and fluorescence collection. Enter the temperatures AND hold times. This is
      a specified teaching assay using SYBR Green, not a universal recipe. A real
      assay requires validated primer, reagent and instrument instructions. Select
      a field for its explanation.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_ValidateProtocol
    Caption: Review every field, then press Validate Protocol.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_ValidateProtocol
    Caption: 'WHY PROGRAM THE RUN?

      The programme controls temperature, duration
      and fluorescence collection. Enter the temperatures AND hold times. This is
      a specified teaching assay using SYBR Green, not a universal recipe. A real
      assay requires validated primer, reagent and instrument instructions. Select
      a field for its explanation.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_StartRun
    Caption: Press Start Run on the instrument after validation passes.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_StartRun
    Caption: 'WHY VALIDATE BEFORE START?

      Confirm identity, seal, orientation,
      seating, closed drawer and every programme entry. The run repeatedly heats
      and cools reactions and measures fluorescence. A wrong setting can cause failed
      or misleading results. Real experiments also require appropriate controls and
      replicates.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_ObserveAmplification
    Caption: Watch the cycle number, temperature stage and fluorescence curves until
      the run finishes.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_ObserveAmplification
    Caption: 'WHAT IS HAPPENING?

      At 95 degrees C the DNA strands separate.
      At 60 degrees C primers bind; fluorescence is collected. At 72 degrees C the
      polymerase extends DNA. SYBR Green reports double-stranded DNA, including unwanted
      products. Watch the blue positive control, green teaching sample and orange
      NTC. Time is accelerated and all curves are synthetic teaching data.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_InterpretResults
    Caption: Answer the three screen questions about controls, Cq and the melt curve.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_InterpretResults
    Caption: 'HOW DO WE READ THE RESULT?

      Cq (also called Ct) is the cycle
      at which fluorescence crosses a chosen threshold above background. The positive
      control should amplify; the NTC contains no template and should not show target
      amplification. Inspect melt peaks for unexpected products. One peak supports
      consistency but does not prove target identity. These checks do not replace
      full assay acceptance criteria.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Qpcr_Complete
    Caption: You have inspected, loaded, configured, run and interpreted a qPCR experiment.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Qpcr_Complete
    Caption: 'WHAT HAVE YOU LEARNED?

      You checked the handoff, aligned and
      loaded a sealed plate, programmed temperatures and hold times, monitored fluorescence
      and interpreted controls, Cq and a melt peak. Keep post-PCR plates sealed to
      reduce carryover contamination. Use the laboratory''s validated procedure for
      disposal and real experiments.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_WearLabCoat
    Caption: Pick up the lab coat and bring it to the wear zone in front of your
      chest.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_WearLabCoat
    Caption: A lab coat helps protect clothing and skin from splashes. This chest
      gesture represents putting it on; it does not reproduce every real dressing
      movement.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_TurnOnTap
    Caption: Press the tap handle to turn on the water.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_TurnOnTap
    Caption: Wet bare hands with clean running water before applying soap. Gloves
      go on only after washing and drying.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_WetHands
    Caption: Place both hands under the running water.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_WetHands
    Caption: Wet bare hands with clean running water before applying soap. Gloves
      go on only after washing and drying.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_UseSoap
    Caption: Press the soap dispenser once.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_UseSoap
    Caption: Soap and rubbing help loosen dirt and microorganisms so they can be
      rinsed away.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_RubHands
    Caption: Rub your hands together for 20 seconds. Clean palms, backs, between
      fingers and under nails. Keep moving both hands close together.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_RubHands
    Caption: Scrub for at least 20 seconds. Clean palms, backs of hands, between
      fingers and under nails. The simulation detects relative hand movement, not
      complete real-world washing technique.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_RinseHands
    Caption: Place both hands under the water again to rinse them.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_RinseHands
    Caption: Rinse away soap and loosened contamination with running water.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_TurnOffTap
    Caption: Press the tap handle to turn off the water.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_TurnOffTap
    Caption: Stop the water when finished. In a real lab avoid recontaminating clean
      hands and follow the local tap and hygiene procedure.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_DryHands
    Caption: Place both hands beneath the hand dryer.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_DryHands
    Caption: Dry hands before putting on gloves. This dryer gesture represents drying;
      follow the drying method specified by your laboratory.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_WearLeftGlove
    Caption: Pick up the left glove with your left hand.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_WearLeftGlove
    Caption: Wear clean, appropriately sized gloves to help prevent contact and contamination.
      Gloves do not replace hand hygiene; change damaged or contaminated gloves.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_WearRightGlove
    Caption: Pick up the right glove with your right hand.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_WearRightGlove
    Caption: Wear clean, appropriately sized gloves to help prevent contact and contamination.
      Gloves do not replace hand hygiene; change damaged or contaminated gloves.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Ppe_Complete
    Caption: PPE complete. You may continue to the qPCR plate inspection.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Why_Ppe_Complete
    Caption: Your coat, handwashing and gloves are complete. These are training checks,
      not a substitute for your laboratory's approved safety procedures.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_ReactionVolume
    Caption: 'Reaction volume


      Reaction volume is the amount in each well,
      not the whole plate. Enter 20 microlitres to match this prepared plate and
      the instrument''s thermal settings.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_LidTemperature
    Caption: 'Lid temperature


      The heated lid is set to 105 degrees C in this
      lesson. It helps limit condensation above the reactions. The lid temperature
      differs from the block temperature.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_InitialDenaturation
    Caption: 'Initial denaturation


      The initial hold is 95 degrees C for 120
      seconds. It separates DNA strands; activation requirements depend on the chosen
      polymerase. Use the validated reagent protocol.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_AmplificationCycles
    Caption: 'Amplification cycles


      Repeat the thermal sequence 35 times.
      Fluorescence is sampled each cycle. More cycles do not automatically mean a
      better or more reliable result.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_Denaturation
    Caption: 'Denaturation


      Denaturation is 95 degrees C for 15 seconds in
      each cycle. Heating separates the DNA strands so primers can bind in the next
      stage.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_Annealing
    Caption: 'Annealing


      Annealing is 60 degrees C for 30 seconds in this
      teaching assay. Primers bind to complementary DNA. The appropriate temperature
      depends on primer design and assay validation.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_Extension
    Caption: 'Extension


      Extension is 72 degrees C for 30 seconds here. The
      DNA polymerase extends the primers to make new strands. Required time depends
      on the enzyme and product length.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_FluorescenceAcquisition
    Caption: 'Fluorescence acquisition


      Acquire fluorescence at 60 degrees
      C during annealing in each cycle. Comparing readings at the same stage provides
      a consistent amplification trace.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_MeltCurveStart
    Caption: 'Melt curve start


      After amplification, increase temperature
      from 65 to 95 degrees C in 0.5-degree steps. DNA melting changes SYBR fluorescence.
      A derivative peak shows a melting transition; extra peaks can indicate unwanted
      products. Dwell time follows the real instrument''s method.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_MeltCurveEnd
    Caption: 'Melt curve end


      After amplification, increase temperature from
      65 to 95 degrees C in 0.5-degree steps. DNA melting changes SYBR fluorescence.
      A derivative peak shows a melting transition; extra peaks can indicate unwanted
      products. Dwell time follows the real instrument''s method.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_TemperatureIncrement
    Caption: 'Temperature increment


      After amplification, increase temperature
      from 65 to 95 degrees C in 0.5-degree steps. DNA melting changes SYBR fluorescence.
      A derivative peak shows a melting transition; extra peaks can indicate unwanted
      products. Dwell time follows the real instrument''s method.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_PostRunHold
    Caption: 'Post-run hold


      The block holds at 4 degrees C after the programme
      in this lesson. This is a short post-run hold, not a long-term storage method.
      Keep amplified plates sealed and follow the disposal procedure.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_InitialHoldSeconds
    Caption: 'Initial hold time


      The initial hold is 95 degrees C for 120
      seconds. It separates DNA strands; activation requirements depend on the chosen
      polymerase. Use the validated reagent protocol.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_DenaturationSeconds
    Caption: 'Denaturation time


      Denaturation is 95 degrees C for 15 seconds
      in each cycle. Heating separates the DNA strands so primers can bind in the
      next stage.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_AnnealingSeconds
    Caption: 'Annealing time


      Annealing is 60 degrees C for 30 seconds in
      this teaching assay. Primers bind to complementary DNA. The appropriate temperature
      depends on primer design and assay validation.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Parameter_ExtensionSeconds
    Caption: 'Extension time


      Extension is 72 degrees C for 30 seconds here.
      The DNA polymerase extends the primers to make new strands. Required time depends
      on the enzyme and product length.'
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Lobby_Welcome
    Caption: Welcome to the T5 XR Genomics Laboratory. Enter a nickname, then select
      Continue.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
  - Key: Instruction_Lobby_Practice
    Caption: Press Practice Select. Then grip the teal practice object, lift it,
      and release your grip.
    Clip: {fileID: 0}
